  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Smoky skies the rest of the day Sunday will \nslightly reduce visibility and degrade air quality.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...Smoke over the area at present is\ncoming mostly from wildfires way northwest of us in the \nNorthwest Territories of Canada. Many models are not \ninitializing the smoke at all, most likely because of its \ntrajectory coming from extremely far north in northern Canada, \nwhich is beyond the domain for many models. An exception is the \nECMWF, which is handling the smoke very well. The ECMWF has the \nsmoke mostly exiting to the east by midnight tonight. \n\nFor the rest of the afternoon and evening, look for hazy skies\nand a slight smell of smoke, with air quality at moderate\nlevels. The thickest smoke is above the ground, but a bit of\nsmoke is making it to the ground. Don't expect the full extent\nof the smoke to mix to the ground though, as the afternoon \nmixing from aloft today is fairly limited.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain moves in late tonight from west to east. \nLocally heavy rainfall may occur late tonight and Monday \nmorning, but flooding is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA soaking rainfall is in store for many areas. Though there\ncould be a few light showers or sprinkles late this afternoon\nand early this evening, the soaking rain kicks in around dawn\nMonday. The rain could be fairly heavy at times especially from\nabout 5am to noon. The rain becomes a bit more off and on and \nshowery in nature later in the day and into Monday evening.\nThere could be an embedded rumble of thunder Monday, but most of\nthe instability is aloft and don't expect anything remotely\nstrong. For rainfall totals from early Monday through Monday\nevening, feel pretty confident in at least one quarter inch for\nmost areas. However, pinpointing the heavier axis of rain where\nup to around an inch could fall is a bit tougher, as models are\nstill in poor agreement. In general, look for one quarter to\nthree quarters of an inch of rain around the area with local\namounts around an inch. This won't be enough to cause flooding.\nRain begins tapering off for good late Monday night, though we\ncould have some fog developing.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another round of rain Wednesday afternoon into\nThursday as a cold front crosses the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the low pressure system exits over the Maritimes, the next\nlow pressure system centered over the Hudson Bay begins to track\nE. The associated cold front is expected to move into the region\nWednesday evening and exit Thursday afternoon. Ensemble models\nhave been trending towards a drier solution with isolated\nshowers. Little QPF is expected with these showers. There is a\npossibility for a few rumbles of thunder but nothing severe.",
